    "title": "Earth Day is April 22: How it started and why it still matters",
    "text": "WASHINGTON (7News) — Every April 22, the planet gets its moment to be in the spotlight with Earth Day.Listen, this isn’t just about planting a tree or posting a pretty sunset on social. It’s a global reminder that this big blue marble we call home needs a little TLC from the people living on it.What Is Earth Day?Think of it as the world’s largest environmental check-in. More than a billion people across over 190 countries take part each year—cleaning up parks, reducing waste, learning something new about the environment, or simply spending time appreciating nature.It’s half celebration, half wake-up call.How It Started?It goes back to 1970 when Gaylord Nelson, a U.S. senator from Wisconsin who thought: we can do better. Inspired by the energy of student activism at the time, he helped organize a nationwide “teach-in” about environmental issues.On April 22, 1970, about 20 million Americans—roughly 10% of the U.S. population at the time—showed up. That massive turnout helped spark major change, including the creation of the Environmental Protection Agency and key laws like the Clean Air Act.Why It Still MattersEarth Day isn’t stuck in the past—it’s more relevant than ever. The challenges are bigger, more global, and more urgent.We’re talking about:Climate change Extreme weatherPlastic pollutionBiodiversity lossFrom school kids planting pollinator gardens to cities committing to cleaner energy, Earth Day shows how small actions can scale into real impact.The vibe of Earth Day todayBring a reusable bagSkip the plastic water bottlePlant something (even if it’s just herbs on a windowsill)Learn one new thing about the planetEven a simple walk outside can help you connect with our planet. Notice all things around you, that's where the connection starts.The big takeawayEarth Day began as a protest. And now, it’s a yearly reminder that the planet doesn’t need a grand gesture—it needs consistent care.You can still celebrate Earth Day this year on April 22nd and even this weekend. The American Indian Smithsonian Museum is hosting the Living Earth Festival on Saturday from 11 a.m. - 5 p.m. Admission is free.",
